          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a defective return notice under Section 139(9)?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A notice from CPC Bengaluru informing that your filed ITR contains errors, missing details or inconsistencies preventing proper processing. It is NOT a penalty - it is a chance to correct. Common defects: wrong ITR form, income mismatch with 26AS/AIS, missing schedules, unverified return, TDS discrepancies. Must rectify within 15 days or return becomes invalid. Patron analyzes and rectifies within 24-48 hours."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I respond to a 139(9) notice?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Log in to incometax.gov.in > Pending Actions > e-Proceedings. Select notice. Choose Agree (correct defect, upload rectification JSON) or Disagree (submit written explanation). For Agree: download offline ITR utility, correct return, generate JSON, upload via portal. Enter CPC notice number and date. Pay self-assessment tax if additional liability. Submit and save acknowledgment."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What happens if I don't respond within 15 days?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Return treated as invalid - equivalent to not having filed. Late filing penalty S.234F (Rs 5,000), interest S.234A (1%/month on unpaid tax), loss of carry-forward of losses (business, capital), loss of specific deductions requiring timely filing, and refund not processed. AO may condone delay if rectified before assessment completion, but this is discretionary."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are the most common defects for Pune taxpayers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "IT professionals (Hinjewadi/Kharadi): dual Form 16 income not consolidated, ESOP perquisite missing from salary schedule, ITR-1 used instead of ITR-2 when capital gains exist. Business owners (Pimpri-Chinchwad): balance sheet and P&L missing in ITR-3. Property investors (Wakad/Baner): incomplete capital gains Schedule CG. All categories: return not e-verified within 30 days."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I file a revised return instead of responding to 139(9)?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Filing revised return under S.139(5) is generally not recommended as primary response because it leaves the 139(9) notice unresolved on e-Proceedings. Recommended: respond to 139(9) directly via portal. However, if defect requires complete ITR form change (e.g., ITR-1 to ITR-2), you may need revised return. In that case, also respond to 139(9) indicating revised return filed."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is defective return notice the same as income tax scrutiny?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Fundamentally different. Section 139(9) is a correction opportunity - CPC identified errors and asks you to fix. No investigation. Section 143(2) scrutiny is detailed AO examination leading to additions, disallowances, increased tax demand. Receiving 139(9) is common and routine; does not indicate tax evasion."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I open the password-protected notice?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Password format: PAN in lowercase + DOB in DDMMYYYY. Example: PAN ABCDE1234F, DOB 15/06/1985 = password abcde1234f15061985. Notice also viewable on Income Tax portal under Pending Actions > e-Proceedings (no password needed on portal)."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
